> At the risk of really, really, embarassing myself, I'll say the quote
> was from Voltaire, and I don't THINK he was a military officer.
>
> OK people, show me how ignorant I am....

The phrase was coined by a late nineteenth or early twentieth century
biographer of Voltaire, whose name I can't remember.  I don't know
whether she expressed herself so badly in that book that it looked as
though she was saying that Voltaire actually said it, or if she was
clear but popular misconceptions set in later.

In any event, she's on record as saying later that the phrase was
intended only to convey the spirit of Voltaire feelings regarding
freedom of speech, and not as an actual quote from the man himself.

On the other hand, here are some Voltairisms which, according to the
incredibly scrupulous journalist and writer George Seldes, are genuine:

    I know many books which have bored their readers, but I know of none
    which has done real evil.

    The truths of religion are never so well understood as by those who
    have lost their power of reasoning.

    The man who says to me, "Believe as I do, or God will damn you,"
    will presently say, "Believe as I do, or I shall assassinate you."

Source: "The Great Quotations," George Seldes, Citadel Press, 1983.
Seldes cites Voltaire's "Philosophical Dictionary" (1764) as the source
for the first two; he gives no cite for the third.

-- William December Starr <wdstarr@crl.com>


