The only method to ask the court for an opinion is to put a case before them --
e.g. "The Crown vs. Lt. Starbuck". Lt. Starbuck was given an opportuntity to
test the case and decided that it would be in his best interests to not press
the issue to a court case.

Although the courts are people who make the final decision everyone, including
Eddie Greenspan, may offer an opinion. It was the opinion of the local police
that Lt. Starbuck was breaking the ban and they were prepared to take it to
court. Given that it seems to be substantially the same circumstances as the
Gordon Domm case (he was charged and convicted) IMHO I agree with them.  Lt.
Starbuck agreed as well -- that may be one reason why he decided to cooperate.

Everyone has a right to offer an opinion. Those who wish to test their opinions
before the courts are free to do so. On the Karla Homolka issue I think you'd
be very foolish to go to court -- there are many more important issue to argue
about.  But feel free. Eddie Greenspan will defend you provided you have very
deep pockets.  

