>>[A satirical piece poking fun at Warwick University's apparent
>>disrespect of freedom of expression, especially satirical expression.]
>>
>>>My department has been notified by Warwick University that the above
>>>message caused great offence, both to Denis Antony personally and to the
>>>University in general.
>>
>>By contacting your department, Warwick U. confirms its disrespect for
>>freedom of expression (especially satirical expression).
>
>Does Warwick University not have the right of reply?

That's a good question, David.  I would suggest that Warwick does, indeed,
have a right of reply - within the forum itself.  As so many have so often
said, one fights speech with speech.  If Warwick had placed a rebuttal or
commentary in the forum(s) to which the satire had been posted, I don't be-
lieve that any uproar would have ensued.  By escalating the issue to the
realms of officialdom and 'legality,' it would seem that Warwick "jumped 
the gun" to some extent.

Consider a (hypothetical) public meeting in which I disagree with a major 
administrator of this university.  Does he have the 'right of reply' to 
rebut my comments?  Absolutely!  Is it (or, better yet, *should* it be) 
proper for that person to call someone in my chain of command and make
the typical "something should be done about this person" comments?  I 
think not.  

Legal action is certainly a valid recourse in many cases; I think, how-
ever, that it was invoked a bit too early in this particular case.  I
recently spent some *serious* effort to convince a few university ad-
ministration that there was no sense of "institutional responsibility"
in these online forums - that each author/speaker was NOT speaking on
behalf of their host system unless an explicit indication of such rep-
resentation was present.  This incident seems to fly in the face of
that assumption.

--Wes

