strange@tezcat.com (Doctor Strange), addressing someone else, writes

>I asked you to define "public forum" and to tell me how the internet is 
>a public forum.
[...]

The term "public forum" is ambiguous. When I used the term, I meant
  * a government-owned forum in the U.S. opened to some of the public
Under this definition, neither the Internet-as-a-whole or Usenet-as
a-whole is a public forum. However, parts of Internet and parts of
Usenet (e.g. the Usenet facilities at the U. of Illinois) likely are
public forums.

A reasonable, but different definition of "public forum" would be
  * any forum (anywhere in the world, managed by anyone)
    opened to some of the public
Under this definition, the Internet-as-a-whole or Usenet might be
considered public forums. I don't, however, think that they any legal
consequence follow from this broad definition of public forum.

That is not to say that this broad definition has no use. Even if it
implies no legal consequences, it may imply moral consequences. These,
however, are harder to argue.

- Carl
