While this may have been true in the distant past (about 2 years in
computer terms) it is not strictly true today.  NIC is placing
restrictions on who may get IP addresses based on wheather or not they
have some sort of government affiliation.  NFSnet itself (which provides
the main backbone for most of the internet users) is moving towards
being restricted to purely research traffic.  Network service providers
are scrambling to get other backbones in place for the day when the
current internet is not available as a "public forum."    Universities and
corporations are also restricting how people get onto their networks.
It used to be that you could walk into the lab at Pitt, wheather you
were a student or not, get an account and get onto the net.  Now you
have to be a student and pay a $200/term fee for net access.

: Moreover, most of the Internet is funded directly or indirectly from government sources. 

Also not entirely true.  We (CDI) tap in off of PREPnet, who had to raise
their rates because NFSnet is now charging non-university providers (ie
corporations) a fee for time on the ether.  I would also be willing to
bet that a significant portion of the routers and machines, as well as
the copper for the signals has not been bought with government grant
money at this point.  As time goes on, the ammount
of tax dollars going into the net will shrink.  Sure, they government
will always provide some equipment to the net (they use it after all,
and they fund a lot of university research)
but I wouldn't be surprised to see most of the net and all of the major
backbones funded by private money within the next 2-3 years.

:  The fact that Netcom has an NNTP server does not make Usenet there a private forum there 
: and a public forum elsewhere.  The people at Netcom have no right to tell others what 
: they can or cannot post in groups that have distribution outside of Netcom just because 
: they consider Usenet to be a private forum there.

But they can determine how their equipment (ie the computer they use to
tie into the net) may be used.  They own it after all.  Isn't one of th
famous sayings "He who pays the piper calls the tune?" (Bear with me
as I go off on one of my semi-relevant tangents for a moment.)  You made a
statement a few days ago that private companies have no say about what 
employees do in their free time.  You're right, unless the employee is using 
company equipment.  If a company provides an employee with equipment to do a 
job during business hours, that company does not have to allow the employee
to use the equipment in whatever way he/she wants to in the "off hours."
For example, do construction companies allow their employees to take
home heavy equipment do dig out a pool during the off hours?  

I doubt there are too many people out there who actually tap into the net
from a T1 line in their house, so a majority of us are either using
company equipment (as evidenced by my .com address) or university
equipment (as evidenced by the .edu on this account's address, and your
own FSU account.)  While the net itself may be publicly funded, the
