This is being posted both to the MUD and the censorship newsgroups
for the purpose of stimulating debate on the propriety of censorship
within Multi-User Dungeons (MUDs).  For those on the censorship
newsgroup who don't know about MUDs, the following is from the MUD
FAQ:

"A MUD...is a computer program which users can log into and explore.
Each user takes control of a computerized persona/avatar/incarnation/
character.  You can walk around, chat with other characters, explore
dangerous monster-infested areas, solve puzzles, and even create your
very own rooms, descriptions, and items."

I might add that many MUDs are based on the Dungeons and Dragons role
playing game.

MUDs are typically run on servers that are controlled by "imps"
(implementors), and people telnet in to the server in order to play.

To begin debate, let's consider a policy that is set by the imps
on the Five Worlds MUD.  Five Worlds' server is within the Boston
University network domain (anybody can play by telnetting to
marble.bu.edu 7777).  Boston University is a private university.
Fingering the server yields the following message:

"This machine is owned and operated by individual students and supporting
members of the marble/3K/Foothills Community."

As part of its environment, Five Worlds features a bulletin board to which
players may post.  It has a post office in which players may send eachother
mail.  It also allows various sorts of interactive chat.  A player may
talk directly to another player, to everyone in the same "room", to
everyone
in the same "group", or on one of several broadcast channels, including
"gossip", "auction", etc.

The Five Worlds' censorship policy is available to any player who types
"help plug":

> help plug
> ADVERTISING PLUG
> 
> If you advertise for another mud you will be dealt with harshly.
> Up to and including being brutally slayed..depending on who catches you.
> 
> You have been warned.

The "advertising" mentioned here is not (yet) commercial advertising.  MUDs
are, by and large, free.

The following questions come to mind:

- If Five Worlds were run on a publicly owned machine in the United States,
would the policy be unconstitutional?

- Does Boston University have anti-censorship policies, and if so would
they
govern Five Worlds?

- Granting that the Five Worlds imps are entirely within their legal and
university rights, are there moral arguments against their censorship?

- Many consider MUDs to be nothing more than games.  In a role playing
game,
if you discuss something outside of the game, that's a violation of game
rules.  Punishment (within the game) is entirely appropriate under this
view.  True?

- Again, taking the game point of view, it's just a game.  Is it just
silly to make a big deal out of censorship, or any other aspect of game
rules for that matter?

- Others consider MUDs to be much more than games.  They are places where
people interact and make friends (some people who have met in MUDs have
gotten married).  Under this view is anything different?

- Some consider MUDs to be places where the human spirit can expand and
push new boundaries.  Fantasies can be explored, people are free to act
without many of the constraints of real life, new worlds can be created.
Again, does this change things?

-- 
