Being that I doctored up the bulk of the censorship policy, it falls
on my shoulders whether my own views are to be considered "moral"
or "immoral" with regard to the censorship on 5W.  I've gotten my 
share of spam on 5W for perhaps siding on the relatively fringe topics
of gay/lesbian rights, racism, etc., topics that you would find
at any major US university.  Sometimes it gets a little uncomfortable
on the 5W gossip line when we chat about gays in the military,
anti-semitism, etc.  It is usually the 5W players that steer the
conversation to whatever level seems appropriate at the time.  When I am 
on, I make an attempt to separate "discussion" from name-calling, derogatory
flaming, etc.  I try to make it educational.  Sometimes I succeed,
sometimes I take the role of PC-police-anal-retentive-weenie.  It's
a rough call (being God is really a pain in the ass) -- 
but there are real-life faces behind the players, and like it or not, 
people do bring real-life experiences into the mud.  
I think I have a responsibility to the folks who play the game to ensure
that their recreation on the mud be both excellent and satisfying.
It's a matter of pride really.  I don't ascribe to misogynism, bigotry, 
ignorance, etc.  Russ Taylor (Alander to you folk) had a great policy 
back on ROM which basically nuked the sexist pig-dogs who referred
to women as play-things/objects/whatever.  He "championed" the 
intelligent over the dumb which kinda angered many in the way he imp'd it,
but many of the "women players" (on MUD you can never tell) liked the idea.
Gone were the over-hormoned males that used muds to abuse women with 
sexual suggestion.  ROM didn't hurt any player-wise (they still had over 
130+ players).  

There are gays, lesbians, Americans, blacks, whites, women, men,
etc. that do play, and on 5W we try to keep things on the peaceable
levels.  People chat and edify themselves on the socio-cultural level 
-- that's probably IMHO the most satisfying part of mudding.
The fact that we bring policy to keep inflammatory speech to a minimum
on MUDs is no more immoral (I guess) than the numerous "fighting words"
policies at universities such as UC Berkeley, UMass, etc.
 
>
>- Again, taking the game point of view, it's just a game.  Is it just
>silly to make a big deal out of censorship, or any other aspect of game
>rules for that matter?
>
Sure it's just a game.  People can decide whether to play or not to play.
I want people to play 5W.  I want those who don't want to play it
to dislike it for the _code_ -- maybe the fidos are too dumb, etc. --
not because of something someone said.

I take it personally if they dislike 5W because someone said "nigger"
or "hated fags because they talk funny or look too much like David 
Copperfield."  I was on another mud to visit some folks and heard 
derogatory remarks regarding homosexuals and at the time, the imps were 
present.  The imps themselves were chiming in about "fags" and their 
habits on the chat line, and I said I found the remarks intolerant and 
uncalled for.  They told me what I tell everyone who thinks 5W speech 
policy is overly sensitive: "Look, if you don't like it, don't play."

It's kinda sad, though, to think that even the imps of this highly 
respected mud allowed this kind of discussion and even shared the 
narrow (IMHO) views with regard to that topic (homosexuality), but what 
can I do?  I can't get them to open up their eyes, or at least consider 
the way I see the world.  Maybe I'm a bit presumptuous and bold in thinking
I see things in the right way and everyone is off-kilter.  There are muds 
out there where you can get a "piece of ass" off "Mariah Carey" if you kill 
her, and lord knows what other else.  Each MUD is in effect, a bully 
pulpit, and well, there are those "piece of ass" and "Fags are freaks" 
muds, and somewhere on the left side, there is 5W.  5W policy makes us 
look like we are on the PC-moral-high-ground/low-ground.  So be it.  And 
people can always take their pick.   

>- Others consider MUDs to be much more than games.  They are places where
>people interact and make friends (some people who have met in MUDs have
>gotten married).  Under this view is anything different?
>
Replace MUDS with UNIVERSITIES and "games" with "school." :)

>- Some consider MUDs to be places where the human spirit can expand and
>push new boundaries.  Fantasies can be explored, people are free to act
>without many of the constraints of real life, new worlds can be created.
>Again, does this change things?
