How could you possibly consider this unconstitutional?  Constitutionality
is a question of law.  The IMPS have not created any federal or state
laws which limit your free speech.  In fact you are free to speak of
anything you want to on the MUD (with possible, and minor repercussions).
If I were to say that if you start talking about Gay rights, I'll make
stupid faces at you, would you consider that a violation of your 
constitutional rights?  It isn't as though killing your character were
against the law, or even any more than a minor inconvenience.

>
>- Does Boston University have anti-censorship policies, and if so would
>they
>govern Five Worlds?
>
It seems to me that you would be hard pressed to define the stated
FW policy as censorship.  Who is reviewing the material?  How can
you identify that person as an official?  If there is an official 
MUD censor who looks for references to other MUDs in material 
published within the domain of the MUD, I would say that is an 
aspect of the imaginary world that was created there, not one of
the real world.  

In other words, since killing you violently within the MUD has no
impact on your Real-Life ability to get your message across, why
would you consider the action to be censorship?

>- Granting that the Five Worlds imps are entirely within their legal and
>university rights, are there moral arguments against their censorship?
>
One supposes that the MUD policies are developed based on the morals
of the Imps, rather than the morals of the network community as a whole.
As far as the actual morals go, I can easily imagine myself creating
an imaginary domain in which censorship plays a dominant role.  I 
think it would be absurd in Real Life to require that no one create
an imaginary domain which makes use of or explores the effects of
censorship.

