Without quoting previous articles to death, the original poster did not
mention all the forms of punishment that can result from breaking the rules.
An imp can not only kill the offending player (his character of course) but
he can also nochannel him (make him unable to do anything but say things to
other people in the same room and use tells) notell him (obvious), disconnect
him from the mud, delete his character, and finally ban his site from ever
logging onto the mud.  This code is in ROM2 which is available for public
ftping.  So, my question is, would banning a site for the actions of one
(or more) people from that site be considered discrimination?  or 
censorship?  IMHO, no.  I'm not sure of the legalities of this question but
it seems to me that no matter who owns the machine the mud is running on, it
is the implementors of the code that make the rules.  If someone wanted to
run a mud where you could only talk in slang, then all they would have to
do is write the code(or modify) and then find a site.  I don't see that the
government has any business coming in and forcing the implementors to change
their code or to shut down the mud (unless it is the owners of the machine
that shut down the mud).  A MUD is a program written by individuals and run
and maintained by individuals, not by the government.  People who log into a
mud have no access to any other part of the machine so it is a lot like
inviting people over to your home to play dungeons and dragons or any other
game.  If you don't like what they are doing, you can kick them out of your
home so the port on that machine that the mud is using is like that.  MUDs
are generally not based on real life and the rules within can vary widely
and are enforceable by the players and immortals of that mud, they are not
state or federal laws and in fact the people who play muds come from all over
the world, so which nation's laws would apply?  think about that.

