news by normal, unstoppable freedom of private speech.  But it was EASY
to forbid the small, easily controlled group of commercial media (the
papers, mags, radio, TV) from doing something on pain of fines.  And that
kept the larger public from learning things.  Now, every citizen can
be handed the power to reach the same audience as the Globe&Mail.  There
is no buffer anymore.  You can't stop the forbidden news from reaching
a large audience without draconian laws that require every last citizen
with a $300 PC and a modem to be as responsible as a newspaper publisher
with decades of experience -- laws that will be seen most inimical to
freedom of expression.

I speak, of course, of laws that require every sysop to use platoons
of staff or AI searches of text or something to watch every mailing list.

Otherwise, the next "Lt. Starbuck" will be twice as successful and only
a tenth as likely to be caught.  You just use a tree of small mailing
lists, (he tells two friends, and they tell two friends...) and
in a few levels you've reached 100,000 people if they're interested.

There are a lot of smart people on the bench and in the House, all
nasty remarks to the contrary.  I sure they'll think of something.

Or am I?


