I don't think either you know very much about UNIX password cracking.  Attacking
/bin/login isn't particularly productive, unless you know a lot about the
system you're trying to break into.  Now if you can retrieve an /etc/passwd
file (how many people have tested their uucp's to ensure that it cannot
be retrieved, plus lots of other mechanisms to get it?), then things become
MUCH more interesting.

Experience has shown that given Morris's word list, plus an english
dictionary, plus the contents of the GCOS field in the password file,
plus suitable programs, you can break as much as 50% or more of the
passwords on any given system, unless password selection rules are in
force (specifically, passwd changing programs that refuse poorly
selected passwords).

I've written a program that can break *any* randomly selected 6 character
mono-case alphabetic password in less than 18 hours.  And that
was at a measly 5000 cracks/second.  We're running a system now that's
doing 50000 cracks/second, and there are systems that can achieve >1,000,000
per second..

[It's okay, I'm from security ;-)]

