Subject: Theft of free newspapers is a crime


For immediate release                             May 31, 1994

                STEALING FREE NEWSPAPERS IS A CRIME

WASHINGTON, DC - If it wasn't clear before, it is now: stealing free
newspapers is a crime in the state of Maryland. Maryland Governor William
Donald Schaefer signed a law May 26 that explicitly prohibits individuals
>from taking "complimentary" newspapers if done "with the intent to prevent
other individuals from reading the newspapers." Those who violate the law
can be fined, jailed or both. The law is the first of its kind in the country.
        State lawmakers proposed the legislation following a rash of
newspaper theft incidents in Maryland, including the theft of student
newspapers at the University of Maryland-College Park, John Hopkins
University and the University of Maryland-Baltimore County. Although not
related, all of these incidents were the result of student minority groups
upset with particular stories or articles in the newspapers, which the
groups claimed were racist.
        Student Press Law Center Executive Director Mark Goodman applauded
the legislature and the governor of Maryland for taking the problem of
newspaper theft seriously.
        "Too many school administrators have treated newspaper theft as an
insignificant prank, if they reacted to it all," said Goodman. "I hope
this legislation will serve as a wake-up call to those who have forgotten
the importance of free expression on college and university campuses."
        While Maryland is the first state to pass a law directed
specifically at curbing the growing number of newspaper theft incidents,
newspaper thieves in other states can be prosecuted under existing state
theft laws, said Goodman. For example, in February 1988, four students at
the University of Florida were charged with and pleaded "no contest" to
theft charges after they confiscated large quantities of a conservative
campus newspaper.
        The theft of campus newspapers reached an all-time high during the
1993-94 school year. According to a report compiled by the Student Press
Law Center, 35 student newspapers have reported thefts on their campus
since last fall.

