<<arguments you have seen a million times deleted fot brevity>>

I really don't understand why this argument constantly comes up.  It
has been hashed and re-hashed a million times, but the bottom line is
very simple:

A MUD is a private domain run by individuals using code that is their
personal property.  Therefore, legal and even moral arguments concerning
censorship (assuming you *could* bring such legalities to an *international*
forum in the first place) simply do not apply.  

I have the right to stop you from saying things that I deem offensive in my 
*private* domain.  I can kick you out of my house, I can hang up the phone
on you, and I can kick you off my mud.  I make the rules.  If you don't like
them, go to someone else's house, call someone else, get off my mud, and
stop insinuating yourself into my personal space.

This has nothing to do with any rights you may have in public.  While I support
good old freedom of speech, I hardly have to let the skinheads come demonstrate
in my house.


What is so difficult about this concept to understand???


