Dear Readers:

I am sorry.  Perhaps my last post was out of line.  I was venting my
emotions.  For those still listening, I spent the whole day at a friend's
house.  She is absolutely nuts about her cats.  She went on and on about
every little detail of her cats.  We went to the mall, to the movies, ate
out....  All day long it was "Fluffy did this" and "Beepina got into the
closet again..." or "my husband doesn't pet the kids enough!"  It got
tiresome quickly.  Anyway, I took it all in stride with a tight lipped
smile after each of her stories.  Perhaps it sounds like I'm overreacting,
but you just had to be there.  The venom built up in my system--I was
ready to attack.  I plopped down in front of my trusty old PC/XT and fired
my diatribe smack into the middle of a hornet's nest.  My months of
lurking and occasional posts informed me that some people in this
newsgroup were just like this girl.  They would pay!  

Little did I know that I had found the world's most sensitive newsgroup.

Gee whiz!  Some people in this newsgroup have most delicate sensibilities.
I'm afraid that I have completely shattered some egos with my words.  I
have driven countless cat lovers off USENET forever and my userid and
post address are being sent to KILLfile databases all over the world.  The
games some people are playing with my email border on coersive.  And my
postmaster has been sent a copy of my posted thread.  I've seen the word
"netiquette" batted around so much, some people must have it assigned to a
macro.  Do the words "Vigil Ante mob" and "net.cops" ring a bell?  Although
I should have chosen less of a posturing tone, guess what, this is how I
felt about the subject of cat stories and housecats.  If this is offensive
and disruptive to rec.pets.cats, then those offended people are used to one
sided arguments, getting the last word, and always being right.  And to be
fair, I imagine that some of the spicy repartee is backlash from the
alt.tasteless debacle.

and the email--
After reading the first letter, I became indignant.  Then each following
letter became more and more delicious.  By the time I read Cat Stanton's curt
letter I was laughing.  Too bad she can't read this, I'm in her KILLfile. 
She should be in MY KILLfile after reading her saucy thread about cats and
her bedroom antics.  Although I found it amusing, my cat was thoroughly
appalled.  Oops, that was STella, how could I confuse the two?  Excuse me,
I am just a "retard".  (Oh there I go, that group (tongue held for now)
simply begs to be flamed.)

what i like about rec.pets.cats--
I love this group.  I love cats.  I have opinions on what is good for cats
and so does everyone else.  The FAQ is awesome!  It is loaded with the best
info.  If someone has a cat question, this is where I look first.  I recently
gave some information to a friend whose cat has FeLV.  Her cat is doing
well, so cross your fingers.  

my terms--
To show my sincerity, and your distaste for all things catty, I will
respond to this thread no more than two times within each one week period. 
All flames should go to alt.flame of course and my email.  Why so
sanctimonious you ask?  Because one way to keep things cleaned up is to
have a little structure in your newsgroup.  After all, you're a rec.
group, one of the magnificent seven--not an alt. group!
Put an OBligitory Cat story requirement in the Charter so you're S/N ratio
is 0.99   Make sure to change the subject description when a thread
drifts.  Since this newsgroup is said to be high volume, require search
strings in subjects like [STO] for cat stories, [TIP] for cat ideas and
hints, [NEW] for news about cats and their health.  Also make sure your
newsgroup description accurately reflects the true flavor of the stories
within.  These are par for the course on most newsgroups anyway.  And it
is so hard to draw that fine line between offensive and silly.  Don't get
caught crying wolf.  

BTW, the email vote is still out on whether to lynch me or praise
me.  It seems that I am hardly the only one with my views about
precious kitty stories and cooped up cats.  Surprise, most of my praise
comes from across the oceans.  Thanks for the support, I may not come out
alive.  Some value systems turn out to be ethnocentric views and they do
not jibe well on Usenet.
