That word set me thinking about ecryption again.

When encryption algorithms are being studied, it is generally assumed that
the algorithm for encryption is known by the one who is trying to decrypt.
(An encryption algorithm is supposed to be unbreakable even if this is true.)

Thus, if you have some encrypted text, you can (given infinite time) try all
possible texts for the source, and test if the result of encrypting it matches
the given encrypted text.

In many cases, however, the algorithm is not known.  How, then, is the text
deciphered?  If we know that the original message is made of letters, then
we could try many decryption algorithms, and test the results of each for
high alphanumeric content.  If that's how it's done, then I could encrypt my 
message effectively by inserting a non-alphanumeric ASCII code after every
character of my message, and then encrypting.
Is there a better way of decrypting?
(BTW, this applies to the pre-computer age of encryption also.)



