Actually, there are a few minor laws (known as the First and Fourteenth 
amendments to the US Constitution) which place a great many regulations 
on how a government agency, which the U. of Missouri most certainly is, 
can restrict the privileges of citizens of the US and of the sundry states.

A strong case can be made that Usenet/Internet/e-mail access which is 
granted to all students of a government-supported college or university 
is in the category of free speech or free press: that is, a student 
cannot, by the First and Fourteenth amendments, have his or her access to 
them taken away simply for offensive content. As far as I know, this 
reasoning has never been tested in court.



