In article <2ssisq$sjj@crl.crl.com>
wdstarr@crl.com (William December Starr) writes:

> That's the kicker: when free newspapers are taken/stolen wholesale,
> something very much akin to force or intimidation _has_ been used, in
> that the free will of a person who might want to have read the paper has
> been overridden/negated without his consent.  The confrontative
> atmosphere of an in-your-face act like blockade picketing is absent,
> true, but the effective result is the same.

Well, this is where I've had trouble. This wasn't a knee-jerk issue for
me. Is "very much akin to" the same as "is"?

For example, I could legally purchase large quantities of papers (if I
had the bucks) and achieve the same effects. Assorted protest groups
will legally buy books, records, whatever, solely to burn them. And so
on.

There also legitimate reasons to take more than one of something -- I
could be grabbing a copy for a friend, for example. The sign I
proposed, which restricting this, is still probably the best choice.

If I were to lead a picket against a movie theatre, and the bad press
caused the theatre owner to change the film to something less
controversial after a day, I have undoubtedly deprived many people of
the chance to see the movie, yet I have also acted completely within
the law and my rights. This might not be a valid example, because the
movie exists and may still be seen elsewhere. The papers in question
were destroyed, not merely made slightly harder to find.

So, as I said, it's hazy for me. It's right on the boundary, to me,
between legitimate counter-speech (such as writing a letter to a
sysadmin (g)) and outright censorship (use of force to deprive someone
of their right of free expression).
